[THOUGHT] Alcohol use is minimal, diet is unrestricted, and there’s no GI malabsorption history — making nutritional deficiencies like B12 or copper deficiency less likely (though not impossible). The absence of red flags for common secondary causes shifts focus toward structural spinal pathology (e.g., slow-growing intradural tumor like meningioma or ependymoma, especially in the thoracic or conus region) or rare neurodegenerative conditions. Before proceeding to physical exam or testing, I must assess for subtle neurologic signs that may have been overlooked: lower extremity strength (hip flexion, knee extension, ankle dorsiflexion), tone, reflexes (patellar, Achilles), plantar responses, and sensory testing (vibration, proprioception) — all critical to localize level and pattern of involvement.

[TEST] Perform a targeted neurologic physical examination including:  
- Manual muscle testing of hip flexion (L1–L2), knee extension (L3–L4), ankle dorsiflexion (L4–L5), and great toe extension (L5);  
- Patellar and Achilles deep tendon reflexes;  
- Babinski sign;  
- Vibration sense at the medial malleolus and iliac crest;  
- Romberg test.

